## Founding & History
Sinapius Wines was established in 2005 by Tasmanians Linda Morice and her late husband, Vaughn Dell. They purchased the former Golders vineyard, which was originally planted in 1994, in the Pipers River region of northern Tasmania. At the time of purchase, both Linda and Vaughn were 23 or 24 years old. Their goal was to produce high-quality wine with minimal intervention, focusing on the work done in the vineyard. Vaughn Dell passed away suddenly in May 2020 at the age of 39 due to an undiagnosed heart condition. Linda Morice continues to lead the winery, carrying forward the legacy they built together. The name "Sinapius" is a family name from Linda's ancestor, Isabella Sinapius, whose father migrated from Prussia in the early 1800s.

## People
Sinapius Wines is family-owned and operated by Linda Morice. Linda and her late husband, Vaughn Dell, founded the winery. Vaughn Dell was responsible for much of the vineyard establishment, including planting 30,000 vines by hand. After Vaughn's passing in 2020, Linda Morice took on the responsibility of winemaking and winegrowing, with assistance from renowned Tasmanian winemakers Joe Holyman and Peter Dredge for some wines. Their two young daughters, Esmé and Clementine, are also part of the family legacy, with wines named after them. Alex Verbeek is listed as the viticulturalist.

## Vineyard & Site
The Sinapius vineyard is located in the Pipers Brook wine region of Northern Tasmania, above the banks of Pipers Brook. It is situated approximately 5 kilometers inland from the Bass Strait, which provides a maritime influence and a long, even growing season. The vineyard spans 4.3 hectares and is positioned 80-100 meters above sea level. The four distinct vineyard blocks face North-East and East, allowing for early warming of the soils and full sun exposure to optimize grape ripeness and flavor. The soils are volcanic-based, consisting of deep red ferrosols and ironstone gravels with a layer of micaceous quartz siltstone.

The vineyard is modeled on Burgundian plantings, featuring high-density planting. Planting densities range from 1.3m between rows x 1m between vines (7,700 vines/ha) to some sections at 1.2m x 0.75m (11,111 vines/ha). This high-density approach aims for low yields per vine (averaging less than 500 grams per vine) to achieve maximal intensity, concentration, and expression of the site in the wines. The fruiting wire is positioned 40cm off the ground, with an overall canopy height of approximately 1.2 meters. Vines are pruned using the Poussard technique to a single 8-10 bud cane.

Sinapius has planted a diverse array of varietals and clones. Varietals include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Grüner Veltliner, Pinot Blanc, Gewürztraminer, Riesling, Gamay, and Ribolla Gialla. Specific clones planted for Pinot Noir include MV6, 114, 115, 667, 777, Abel, D4V2, UCD5, 386, 521, G5V15, D2V5, and D5V12, Cortaillod. Chardonnay clones include 548, 76, 95, 96, 809, 277, Mendoza, Penfolds 58, I10V1, 352, and 124. Pinot Gris clones are 457, D1V7, D1V10, Grüner Veltliner is F6V16, Pinot Blanc is 54, Gewürztraminer is 457, and Riesling clones are GM110, GM239, 810, 812, 813, 68 Trier, 356 Trier, 156, N90. Gamay clones include 222, 284, BGW19, and H200A. The farming approach is meticulous viticulture, with most work done by hand due to the high-density plantings.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Sinapius cellar door offers an intimate and authentic tasting experience. It is open Thursday, Friday, and Monday from 11 am to 5 pm, and on weekends and public holidays from 12 pm to 5 pm. No tastings are available after 4:30 pm. The cellar door is closed on Tuesdays, Wednesdays, Christmas Day, and Boxing Day. Bookings are recommended for all tasting experiences, especially for larger groups or during peak seasons. Groups and buses are by appointment only. Visitors can sample small-batch, handcrafted wines and learn about the vineyard's unique approach to winemaking, including insights into high-density plantings and sustainable practices. The tasting space is described as a relaxed setting, with indoor and outdoor tasting options and scenic views. The cellar door is a quaint cottage style with a window into the winery. The property provides a picturesque backdrop, set above the banks of Pipers Brook.

## Food & Hospitality
No reliable information found from public sources regarding a restaurant, kitchen, menu style, notable dishes, or separate events beyond wine tasting.

## Awards & Recognition
The 2007 Chardonnay, the first wine made by Vaughn Dell, won top gold in the Tasmanian Wine Show. Sinapius wines have received critical acclaim, with the 2021 Close Planted Pinot Noir and 2021 Close Planted Chardonnay both scoring 94 points from Campbell Mattinson of The Wine Front. James Halliday, a prominent wine critic, has rated Sinapius. The winery has gained a "cult following" and respect from peers. Linda Morice, leading Sinapius, continues to be recognized among Tasmania's finest wines.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Sinapius practices meticulous viticulture and minimal intervention winemaking, with a focus on sustainability. They consider themselves "wine growers" rather than just winemakers, emphasizing that most of the work is done in the vines. Their approach includes natural ferments and minimal additions. No specific organic or biodynamic certifications were found in the search results, but the emphasis is on sustainable practices and expressing the unique terroir.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Sinapius Wines is 4232 Bridport Road, Pipers Brook, Tasmania 7254, Australia. It is located approximately 55km north of Launceston.

Phone: 0488 740 234. An older phone number, 0417 341 764, also appears in some listings.
Website: http://sinapius.com.au/.
Email: cellardoor@sinapius.com.au for cellar door inquiries, and wine@sinapius.com.au for general inquiries.
Social Media: Sinapius has a presence on Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram.

The cellar door is open Thursday, Friday, and Monday from 11 am to 5 pm, and on weekends and public holidays from 12 pm to 5 pm. It is closed on Tuesdays, Wednesdays, Christmas Day, and Boxing Day. Bookings are recommended, especially for groups. The driveway is described as a long, steep gravel road that can be bumpy.

## Notable / Distinctive
Sinapius is genuinely distinctive due to its commitment to high-density vineyard plantings, modeled on Burgundy, which is significantly more intensive than most Australian vineyards. This approach, combined with meticulous hand-tending of vines, results in extremely low yields per vine and concentrated fruit. The winery's philosophy emphasizes being "wine growers" rather than just winemakers, with the belief that the hard work in the vineyard is paramount. They are known for their minimal intervention winemaking, including natural ferments and minimal additions. Sinapius also stands out for its diverse range of varietals, including less common ones like Grüner Veltliner, Ribolla Gialla, and Pinot Blanc, alongside their acclaimed Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The winery's origin story, with founders Vaughn Dell and Linda Morice starting at a young age and Vaughn's sudden passing, adds a poignant layer to its identity, with Linda continuing their shared vision. The naming of wines after their daughters, Esmé and Clementine, further personalizes the brand.

